data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** The syntax is straightforward:

```csharp
Span<nint>  nints  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes; on a 32-bit runtime it would be 4 bytes. The only caveat encountered is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context (since the size isn't known at compile time), but `stackalloc` itself works fine in safe code when assigned to a `Span<T>`.
